================================================================================
Code review — MCP per-domain policy (post-implementation pass)
================================================================================

Context: full code review of the per-domain MCP policy feature against
`.cursorrules` (rule compliance, duplication, dead code, session-data audit),
followed by the unit suite and the opt-in live scenario campaign. Four findings
were actioned; the rest of the diff was found compliant.

Changes:

1. `src/back/core/mcp_tools.py` — removed `disabled_tools()` and
   `context_mode()`. Both were dead: the MCP server cannot import the app
   package and rolls its own `_disabled_tools`, while `NodeContextService`
   reads the already-extracted context sub-dict via
   `context_feature_disabled()`. Neither helper had a production call site;
   only their own unit test exercised them. Dropped the now-unused `List`
   import.

2. `tests/units/core/test_mcp_tools_policy.py` — rewrote the two assertions
   that used the removed helpers so the behavioural coverage is preserved:
   the default lookups are now asserted directly on the coerced policy, which
   is what every real consumer does.

3. `src/mcp-server/server/app.py` — Extract Function `_hint()` for the five
   preferred/neutral wording choices. They were multi-line conditional
   expressions buried inside `lines.append(...)`, with the `if/else` trailing
   several lines of string literal. `_preferred()` is kept (it is asserted
   directly by the formatting suite) and `_hint()` sits on top of it.

4. `src/front/static/global/js/navbar.js` — moved `buildMcpPolicy()` above the
   `buildDomainInfoPayload()` JSDoc block. The new function had been inserted
   between that block and the function it documents, leaving
   `buildDomainInfoPayload()` undocumented and the block attached to the wrong
   function.

5. `documentation/mcp.md`, `documentation/features.md`,
   `documentation/code_organization.md`, `README.md`,
   `.planning/mcp-domain-policy/SPEC.md` — corrected an inaccurate isolation
   claim. The docs stated there was "no leakage between concurrent clients that
   selected different domains". Only the FastMCP *visibility rules* are
   session-scoped: `create_mcp_server()` runs once per process, so
   `_selected_domain` / `_domain_policy` / `_class_actions` are closures shared
   by every session and the last `select_domain` wins for all of them. The
   call-time guard follows that shared selection. This predates the policy (the
   same closure already routed queries) and is now documented as a known
   limitation, with a failure-mode row in the SPEC.

6. `tests/e2e/scenarios/test_scenario_03_rules_analysis.py` — raised the
   default `_DQ_TIMEOUT_S` from 300s to 600s, aligned with `_RULES_TIMEOUT_S`.
   The campaign failed on this budget while the run was at 99%; the app log
   shows `dataquality_checks` completing normally in 5m37s (96 passed, 20
   failed, 1 warning). SHACL resolves property URIs one at a time, so the cost
   scales with the 107 shapes, not the triple count. The budget was the
   outlier among the scenario's five long-running steps.

Reviewed and left unchanged (findings recorded, no edit):

- `Domain.py` repeats `coerce_mcp_policy(self._s.info.get("mcp_policy"))` in
  four methods. This is the file's established idiom — `review_quorum`,
  `graph_backend` and `lakehouse_materialization` are repeated the same way.
  Extracting only the new field would break the local symmetry; the four
  should be extracted together in a separate pass.
- `REGISTRY_TOOLS` is duplicated between `src/mcp-server/server/app.py` and
  `back/core/mcp_tools.py`. Deliberate — the MCP process cannot import the app
  package — and `test_domain_policy_formatting.py` asserts the two stay equal.
- Broad `except Exception` in `_ensure_domains_mcp_policy_column` and in the
  MCP visibility/preload paths. Matches the sibling self-heal methods and is
  commented; the fallback is the pre-policy behaviour.

