================================================================================
Fix — UC function picker: output count, and return columns read from the wrong view
================================================================================

Context: the picker was asked to state how many values each Unity Catalog
function returns. Adding the count surfaced a bug that the unit suite could not
have caught: it read `outputs unknown` for every table function against a real
workspace, and the virtual attribute picker consequently refused all of them
("exposes no usable return column").

Root cause: `_fetch_return_columns` queried `information_schema.parameters`
with the complement of the `parameter_mode = 'IN'` filter, on the assumption
that result columns are rows in that view. They are not — Databricks lists only
the declared *arguments* there, so a `RETURNS TABLE` function contributes no
row for what it returns. Verified live: `parameters` yields exactly one row for
a six-column table function (its single input), while
`information_schema.routine_columns` yields the six result columns with their
types and ordinal positions. The mock in the unit test satisfied the shape of
the old query, which is why it passed.

Changes:

1. `src/back/core/databricks/uc/UnityCatalog.py` — `_fetch_return_columns` now
   joins `information_schema.routine_columns` on the `specific_*` triple. The
   second-statement split is still needed (a single join of arguments and
   result columns multiplies rows), but the reason is now the view, not the
   predicate. Soft-fail behaviour unchanged.
2. `src/front/static/ontology/js/ontology-shared-panels.js` — added the shared
   `_ucOutputCount` / `_ucOutputBadge` helpers and an output-count badge to
   both function pickers (they share the "Select a Unity Catalog Function"
   title). A scalar function counts as 1; a table function whose columns the
   metastore did not report reads `outputs unknown` rather than being shown as
   returning nothing. In the virtual attribute picker the hint also spells out
   the consequence — "creates N attributes" — since that count is what the
   user is really choosing.
3. `tests/units/core/test_unity_catalog.py` — the query-shape test now asserts
   the `routine_columns` source and the absence of `parameters` in that second
   statement, so the same mistake cannot come back silently.
4. `tests/units/front/test_virtual_attributes_ui.py` — two guards: both
   pickers state the count, and the unknown case is stated rather than guessed.

Also created `benoit_cayla.customer.customer_live_standing(p_customer_id)` in
the demo workspace — a six-column table function (active contracts, overdue
invoices, outstanding amount, open claims, average satisfaction, derived
standing) over the existing contract / invoice / claim / call tables. It is the
fixture that exposed the bug above: one input parameter, six outputs, which is
exactly the shape a virtual attribute needs.

